.TH "PERFORMANCECONTAINERTIMING" "3JS" "October 28, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
PerformanceContainerTiming \- PerformanceContainerTiming
.SH SYNOPSIS
The \f[B]\f[CB]PerformanceContainerTiming\f[B]\f[R] interface of the
Performance API represents a single update of the timing information for
the painting of elements within a container.
A developer can observe a stream of these objects to analyze the
rendering behavior of a component built from multiple elements.
.PP
\f[CR]PerformanceContainerTiming\f[R] inherits from
\f[CR]PerformanceEntry\f[R].
.SH INSTANCE PROPERTIES
This interface directly defines the following properties:
.TP
\f[B]PerformanceContainerTiming.firstRenderTime\f[R] \f[I](read\-only)\f[R] \f[I](experimental)\f[R]
Returns a \f[CR]DOMHighResTimeStamp\f[R] representing the time of the
first paint that contributed content to this container.
.TP
\f[B]PerformanceContainerTiming.identifier\f[R] \f[I](read\-only)\f[R] \f[I](experimental)\f[R]
Returns the value of the \f[CR]containertiming\f[R] attribute on the
container\(cqs root element.
.TP
\f[B]PerformanceContainerTiming.intersectionRect\f[R] \f[I](read\-only)\f[R] \f[I](experimental)\f[R]
Returns a \f[CR]DOMRectReadOnly\f[R] representing the bounding
rectangle, within the viewport, of all the content painted in the
container since it was registered as a container root.
.TP
\f[B]PerformanceContainerTiming.lastPaintedElement\f[R] \f[I](read\-only)\f[R] \f[I](experimental)\f[R]
Returns the \f[CR]HTMLElement\f[R] that contributed the largest newly
painted area in the frame that generated this entry, or \f[CR]null\f[R]
if there is none.
.TP
\f[B]PerformanceContainerTiming.paintTime\f[R] \f[I](read\-only)\f[R] \f[I](experimental)\f[R]
Returns the \f[CR]timestamp\f[R] when the rendering phase ended and the
paint phase started.
It is useful for comparing results across browsers.
.TP
\f[B]PerformanceContainerTiming.presentationTime\f[R] \f[I](read\-only)\f[R] \f[I](experimental)\f[R]
Returns the \f[CR]timestamp\f[R] when the container\(cqs newly painted
content was actually drawn on the screen.
The value is implementation dependent, and therefore useful primarily
for comparing results from a single browser.
.TP
\f[B]PerformanceContainerTiming.rootElement\f[R] \f[I](read\-only)\f[R] \f[I](experimental)\f[R]
Returns the \f[CR]HTMLElement\f[R] that is the container root, or
\f[CR]null\f[R].
This is the element to which the \f[CR]containertiming\f[R] attribute
was applied.
.TP
\f[B]PerformanceContainerTiming.size\f[R] \f[I](read\-only)\f[R] \f[I](experimental)\f[R]
Returns an integer representing the cumulative area, in square CSS
pixels, of all the content painted in the container since it was
registered as a container root.
.PP
These inherited \f[CR]PerformanceEntry\f[R] properties are constrained
as described:
.TP
\f[B]PerformanceEntry.duration\f[R] \f[I](read\-only)\f[R] \f[I](experimental)\f[R]
Returns \f[CR]0\f[R] (\f[CR]duration\f[R] does not apply to this
interface).
.TP
\f[B]PerformanceEntry.entryType\f[R] \f[I](read\-only)\f[R] \f[I](experimental)\f[R]
Returns the string \f[CR]\(dqcontainer\(dq\f[R].
.TP
\f[B]PerformanceEntry.name\f[R] \f[I](read\-only)\f[R] \f[I](experimental)\f[R]
Returns the empty string (\f[CR]\(dq\(dq\f[R]).
.TP
\f[B]PerformanceEntry.startTime\f[R] \f[I](read\-only)\f[R] \f[I](experimental)\f[R]
Returns the value of this entry\(cqs \f[CR]paintTime\f[R].
.SH INSTANCE METHODS
.TP
\f[B]PerformanceContainerTiming.toJSON()\f[R] \f[I](experimental)\f[R]
Overrides the \f[CR]PerformanceEntry.toJSON()\f[R] method to return a
JSON representation of the \f[CR]PerformanceContainerTiming\f[R] object.
.SH DESCRIPTION
This interface gives web developers tools to measure rendering
timestamps for groups of elements within a container.
It is useful for measuring when a component built from multiple nested
elements, such as a UI widget, has rendered as a whole.
Observers that track the paint time of individual elements, or the
loading of the whole page, can\(cqt measure this effectively.
.PP
A container that paints its nested elements over multiple frames
generates a sequence of \f[CR]PerformanceContainerTiming\f[R] entries:
at most one per rendering frame.
The sequence ends when the user scrolls or interacts with the page (see
Reporting stops after scrolling or user input).
.PP
Each entry in the sequence reflects a different paint, so the
\f[CR]paintTime\f[R] and inherited \f[CR]startTime\f[R] change from one
entry to the next.
The \f[CR]firstRenderTime\f[R] is the same across every entry reported
for that container root since it was registered.
The \f[CR]intersectionRect\f[R] and \f[CR]size\f[R] describe the
container\(cqs accumulated painted region since it was registered, while
the \f[CR]lastPaintedElement\f[R] indicates the element that contributed
most to the current entry.
.PP
This information allows developers to build up an understanding of when
each part of the container was rendered in each frame.
.SS Registering a container
To observe entries from a container, we add the
\f[CR]containertiming\f[R] attribute to that element, specifying an
identifier for the container.
The element then becomes the \f[I]container root element\f[R], or simply
the \(lqcontainer root\(rq.
Paints of new visible regions by the nested elements (but not the root
element itself) will subsequently contribute to container timing
updates.
.PP
You can set any HTML element as a container, but not SVG or MathML
elements.
Note though that some elements, such as \f[CR]<hr>\f[R] and
\f[CR]<img>\f[R], can\(cqt contain other elements.
You can mark these as container roots, but they won\(cqt generate timing
entries.
.PP
The following HTML shows how a container is registered.
It defines a card\-style component built from an \f[CR]<article>\f[R]
element that contains a number of other elements:
.IP
.EX
<\f[B]article\f[R] containertiming=\(dqcool\-card\(dq>
  <\f[B]header\f[R]>
    <\f[B]img\f[R] src=\(dqavatar.jpg\(dq alt=\(dq\(dq />
    <\f[B]span\f[R] class=\(dqusername\(dq>\(atusername</\f[B]span\f[R]>
  </\f[B]header\f[R]>
  <\f[B]p\f[R]>Chat text.</\f[B]p\f[R]>
  <\f[B]div\f[R] containertimingignore>
    \f[I]<!\-\- third\-party \(dqsuggested for you\(dq widget \-\->\f[R]
  </\f[B]div\f[R]>
</\f[B]article\f[R]>
.EE
.PP
The \f[CR]<article>\f[R] is turned into a container root identified as
\f[CR]\(dqcool\-card\(dq\f[R] by its \f[CR]containertiming\f[R]
attribute.
Paints from its avatar image, username, and chat text all contribute to
the container\(cqs timing updates.
.PP
The nested \f[CR]<div>\f[R] has the \f[CR]containertimingignore\f[R]
attribute, which prevents paints from that element and its descendants
from contributing to the timing updates.
This is useful for excluding content that isn\(cqt essential to the
container from its timing, such as an advertisement displayed inside
your card: the card is usable whether or not the advertisement has
painted.
.PP
Container roots can be nested.
A paint inside an inner container root contributes to the timing of the
inner root and of each of its ancestor container roots.
If the inner container root also has the
\f[CR]containertimingignore\f[R] attribute, its paints contribute only
to its own timing.
.SS Observing container entries
You can observe the entries from \f[CR]\(dqcool\-card\(dq\f[R] using a
\f[CR]PerformanceObserver\f[R], in the same way as for other Performance
API entry types.
Note that in this code fragment we first feature check by testing if
\f[CR]PerformanceObserver.supportedEntryTypes\f[R] includes
\f[CR]\(dqcontainer\(dq\f[R], and we pass
\f[CR]{ type: \(dqcontainer\(dq }\f[R] to \f[CR]observe()\f[R] so that
we get only container timing entries:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]if\f[R] (PerformanceObserver.supportedEntryTypes.includes(\(dqcontainer\(dq)) {
  \f[I]// PerformanceContainerTiming supported\f[R]
  \f[B]const\f[R] observer = \f[B]new\f[R] PerformanceObserver((list) \f[B]=>\f[R] {
    \f[B]for\f[R] (\f[B]const\f[R] entry \f[B]of\f[R] list.getEntries()) {
      console.log(
        \(gaContainer \(dq${entry.identifier}\(dq total painted area ${entry.size}px² at ${entry.startTime.toFixed(1)}ms\(ga,
      );
    }
  });
  observer.observe({ type: \(dqcontainer\(dq, buffered: \f[B]true\f[R] });
} \f[B]else\f[R] {
  console.log(\(dqThis feature is not supported by your browser.\(dq);
}
.EE
.SS When are entries generated?
At most one entry is generated for each container in each rendering
frame.
Entries are generated only for updates from nested elements in the
visual viewport, and only for paint operations that draw over new areas.
Entries are not generated for elements excluded by
\f[CR]containertimingignore\f[R].
They also aren\(cqt generated for elements that aren\(cqt painted (such
as those with \f[CR]display: none\f[R]), or for the container root
element itself.
.PP
The browser maintains a record, which we refer to here as the
\(lqaccumulated painted region\(rq, to track whether painting an element
should generate a container timing entry.
This record is separate from the rendering pipeline.
The first time an element in a container is rendered, the browser clips
the painted area to the visual viewport, and updates the accumulated
painted region to include it.
If the accumulated painted region grows (that is, a new area is
painted), the browser generates a container timing entry.
The \f[CR]PerformanceContainerTiming.intersectionRect\f[R] property
returns the bounding rectangle of the region, and
\f[CR]PerformanceContainerTiming.size\f[R] returns its painted area.
The area of the \f[CR]intersectionRect\f[R] is always greater than or
equal to the \f[CR]size\f[R], because the rectangle can include gaps
between painted areas.
.PP
Changes that do not cause the accumulated painted region to increase do
not trigger a new entry.
This includes:
.IP \(bu 2
A paint that\(cqs fully clipped away
.IP \(bu 2
A paint of an area that\(cqs already in the region
.IP \(bu 2
A repaint of an element after its first paint
.PP
In addition, entries are not generated for content inside a shadow tree,
even if the container root itself is inside that shadow tree.
Container timing also doesn\(cqt cross frame boundaries: content inside
an \f[CR]<iframe>\f[R] isn\(cqt exposed to a container root in the
parent frame.
.PP
No entries are generated at all after the user scrolls or interacts with
the page, as described in Reporting stops after scrolling or user input.
.SS Reporting stops after scrolling or user input
Entries are reported only until the user first scrolls or interacts with
the page, such as by clicking or pressing a key.
Scrolling includes scrolling any scrollable element in the page, as well
as scrolling the page itself.
After that, no further entries are generated.
.PP
This is because the API is designed to measure how long it takes for
content to appear as it loads.
Once the user has interacted with the page, times measured from the time
origin include time spent by the user, so they are no longer a useful
measure of rendering performance.
.PP
This also means that the accumulated painted region only ever includes
content that was painted inside the viewport before the user scrolled.
Content that is outside the viewport when it is painted, such as content
below the fold, is never reported, even if the user later scrolls it
into view.
The \f[CR]intersectionRect\f[R] and \f[CR]size\f[R] therefore indicate
how much of the container rendered within the viewport, rather than how
much of the whole container rendered.
.SS Container lifecycle
A container root is registered as soon as an element with the
\f[CR]containertiming\f[R] attribute is connected to the document.
Removing the attribute, or disconnecting the element from the document,
deregisters the container root and discards everything it has
accumulated so far.
If the attribute is later re\-added (or the element is reconnected while
the attribute is still present), the container root is re\-registered
with an empty painted region and a new \f[CR]firstRenderTime\f[R] on the
next paint.
The \f[CR]containertiming\f[R] attribute should therefore be set before
the element is added to the document, so that the container\(cqs entries
reflect all of its paints.
.PP
A container\(cqs painted region is also tracked in viewport coordinates:
if the container root moves later, for example due to a layout change,
the rectangles already accumulated for it aren\(cqt retroactively
adjusted to follow it.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Observing container render times
In this example, two containers are marked for observation by adding the
\f[CR]containertiming\f[R] attribute.
A \f[CR]PerformanceObserver\f[R] is registered to get all performance
entries of type \f[CR]\(dqcontainer\(dq\f[R], and the
\f[CR]buffered\f[R] flag is used to access data from before observer
creation.
.SS HTML
First we define two elements that are marked as container roots with the
\f[CR]containertiming\f[R] attribute, identified as
\f[CR]\(dqhero\(dq\f[R] and \f[CR]\(dqproduct\-list\(dq\f[R],
respectively.
.IP
.EX
<\f[B]section\f[R] containertiming=\(dqhero\(dq>
  <\f[B]h2\f[R]>Hero content</\f[B]h2\f[R]>
</\f[B]section\f[R]>
<\f[B]div\f[R] containertiming=\(dqproduct\-list\(dq>
  <\f[B]p\f[R]>Product list content</\f[B]p\f[R]>
</\f[B]div\f[R]>
.EE
.PP
Note that there is also hidden HTML (and code) for displaying log
information.
.SS JavaScript
The following code first checks whether the browser supports
\f[CR]\(dqcontainer\(dq\f[R] entries: if not, it logs that the feature
is not supported.
It then creates a \f[CR]PerformanceObserver\f[R] that logs each
entry\(cqs \f[CR]identifier\f[R], \f[CR]size\f[R], and
\f[CR]startTime\f[R].
.IP
.EX
\f[B]if\f[R] (PerformanceObserver.supportedEntryTypes.includes(\(dqcontainer\(dq)) {
  \f[B]const\f[R] observer = \f[B]new\f[R] PerformanceObserver((list) \f[B]=>\f[R] {
    \f[B]for\f[R] (\f[B]const\f[R] entry \f[B]of\f[R] list.getEntries()) {
      log(
        \(gaContainer \(dq${entry.identifier}\(dq total painted area ${entry.size}px² at ${entry.startTime.toFixed(1)}ms\(ga,
      );
    }
  });
  observer.observe({ type: \(dqcontainer\(dq, buffered: \f[B]true\f[R] });
} \f[B]else\f[R] {
  log(\(dqThis feature is not supported by your browser.\(dq);
}
.EE
.SS Result
The following log shows the \f[CR]identifier\f[R], \f[CR]size\f[R], and
\f[CR]startTime\f[R] reported for each of the two containers.
.SS Observing how entries change as a container grows
This example logs the properties of each entry for a single container,
which grows as new content is added to it on a timer.
.SS HTML
First we define a \f[CR]<section>\f[R] element that is marked as a
container root identified as \f[CR]\(dqhero\(dq\f[R] by its
\f[CR]containertiming\f[R] attribute, along with a button to reset the
example.
.IP
.EX
<\f[B]button\f[R] id=\(dqreset\(dq>Reset</\f[B]button\f[R]>
<\f[B]section\f[R] containertiming=\(dqhero\(dq>
  <\f[B]h2\f[R]>Hero content</\f[B]h2\f[R]>
</\f[B]section\f[R]>
.EE
.PP
Note that there is also hidden HTML (and code) for displaying log
information.
New log entries are added at the top of the log.
.SS JavaScript
The following code first checks whether the browser supports
\f[CR]\(dqcontainer\(dq\f[R] entries: if not, it logs that the feature
is not supported.
It then creates a \f[CR]PerformanceObserver\f[R] that logs a numbered
set of lines for each entry and its properties.
The \f[CR]presentationTime\f[R] is implementation\-dependent, so the
code logs \f[CR]n/a\f[R] when the browser doesn\(cqt provide a value
(the property is \f[CR]null\f[R]).
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] container = document.querySelector(\(dqsection\(dq);
\f[B]let\f[R] entryNumber = 0;

\f[B]const\f[R] formatTime = (time) \f[B]=>\f[R] (time ? time.toFixed(1) : \(dqn/a\(dq);
\f[B]const\f[R] formatElement = (element) \f[B]=>\f[R]
  element ? \(ga<${element.localName}>\(ga : \(dqnull\(dq;

\f[B]if\f[R] (PerformanceObserver.supportedEntryTypes.includes(\(dqcontainer\(dq)) {
  \f[B]const\f[R] observer = \f[B]new\f[R] PerformanceObserver((list) \f[B]=>\f[R] {
    \f[B]for\f[R] (\f[B]const\f[R] entry \f[B]of\f[R] list.getEntries()) {
      \f[B]const\f[R] rect = entry.intersectionRect;
      log(
        [
          \(ga#${++entryNumber} id: ${entry.identifier}, rootElement: ${formatElement(entry.rootElement)}, firstRenderTime: ${formatTime(entry.firstRenderTime)}\(ga,
          \(ga  paintTime: ${formatTime(entry.paintTime)}\(ga,
          \(ga  presentationTime: ${formatTime(entry.presentationTime)}\(ga,
          \(ga  size: ${entry.size}\(ga,
          \(ga  intersectionRect: ${rect.width.toFixed(1)}(w) x ${rect.height.toFixed(1)}(h)\(ga,
          \(ga  lastPaintedElement: ${formatElement(entry.lastPaintedElement)}\(ga,
        ].join(\(dq\(rsn\(dq),
      );
    }
  });
  observer.observe({ type: \(dqcontainer\(dq, buffered: \f[B]true\f[R] });
} \f[B]else\f[R] {
  log(\(dqThis feature is not supported by your browser.\(dq);
}
.EE
.PP
We then use \f[CR]setInterval()\f[R] to add a new paragraph to the
container every second, stopping after five paragraphs.
Each paragraph triggers a new paint and timing entry.
The paragraphs have increasing amounts of text, so the painted area
grows by a different amount each time.
.PP
Note that we add content on a timer rather than when the user clicks a
button, because no entries are reported after the user interacts with
the page.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] maxParagraphs = 5;
\f[B]let\f[R] count = 0;

\f[B]const\f[R] timer = setInterval(() \f[B]=>\f[R] {
  count++;
  \f[B]const\f[R] paragraph = document.createElement(\(dqp\(dq);
  paragraph.textContent = \(gaNew paragraph ${count}. ${\(dqText. \(dq.repeat(count * 3)}\(ga;
  container.appendChild(paragraph);
  \f[B]if\f[R] (count >= maxParagraphs) {
    clearInterval(timer);
  }
}, 1000);
.EE
.PP
Last of all we add a click event handler to reset the example by
reloading the page.
.IP
.EX
document.querySelector(\(dq#reset\(dq).addEventListener(\(dqclick\(dq, () \f[B]=>\f[R] {
  window.location.reload();
});
.EE
.SS Result
A new paragraph is added every second: compare the values in each log
entry as the container grows.
Click the \f[B]Reset\f[R] button to restart the example.
.PP
Note that if you click, press a key, or scroll inside the example, no
further entries are reported until you reset it.
.SS Observing that scrolling stops reporting
This example demonstrates that entries stop being reported for a
container after the user scrolls, even though new content is still being
painted into it.
.SS HTML
First we define a small scrollable \f[CR]<div>\f[R], a button to reset
the example, and a \f[CR]<section>\f[R] element that is marked as a
container root identified as \f[CR]\(dqhero\(dq\f[R] by its
\f[CR]containertiming\f[R] attribute.
.IP
.EX
<\f[B]div\f[R] id=\(dqscroll\-box\(dq>
  <\f[B]p\f[R]>Scroll this box to stop reporting.</\f[B]p\f[R]>
  <\f[B]p\f[R]>Keep scrolling\&...</\f[B]p\f[R]>
  <\f[B]p\f[R]>\&...nearly there\&...</\f[B]p\f[R]>
  <\f[B]p\f[R]>\&...end of the box.</\f[B]p\f[R]>
</\f[B]div\f[R]>
<\f[B]button\f[R] id=\(dqreset\(dq>Reset</\f[B]button\f[R]>
<\f[B]section\f[R] containertiming=\(dqhero\(dq>
  <\f[B]h2\f[R]>Hero content</\f[B]h2\f[R]>
</\f[B]section\f[R]>
.EE
.SS CSS
The CSS gives the \f[CR]<div>\f[R] a fixed height and makes its content
scrollable.
.IP
.EX
scroll\-box {
  \f[B]width\f[R]: 250px;
  \f[B]height\f[R]: 3rem;
  \f[B]overflow\f[R]: auto;
  \f[B]border\f[R]: 1px solid black;
}

section p {
  \f[B]margin\f[R]: 0;
}
.EE
.PP
Note that there is also hidden HTML (and code) for displaying log
information.
New log entries are added at the top of the log.
.SS JavaScript
The following code first checks whether the browser supports
\f[CR]\(dqcontainer\(dq\f[R] entries: if not, it logs that the feature
is not supported.
It then creates a \f[CR]PerformanceObserver\f[R] that logs the
\f[CR]size\f[R] and \f[CR]paintTime\f[R] of each entry.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] container = document.querySelector(\(dqsection\(dq);

\f[B]if\f[R] (PerformanceObserver.supportedEntryTypes.includes(\(dqcontainer\(dq)) {
  \f[B]const\f[R] observer = \f[B]new\f[R] PerformanceObserver((list) \f[B]=>\f[R] {
    \f[B]for\f[R] (\f[B]const\f[R] entry \f[B]of\f[R] list.getEntries()) {
      log(
        \(gaEntry: size: ${entry.size}, paintTime: ${entry.paintTime.toFixed(1)}\(ga,
      );
    }
  });
  observer.observe({ type: \(dqcontainer\(dq, buffered: \f[B]true\f[R] });
} \f[B]else\f[R] {
  log(\(dqThis feature is not supported by your browser.\(dq);
}
.EE
.PP
We then use \f[CR]setInterval()\f[R] to add a new paragraph to the
container every second, stopping after ten paragraphs.
Each paragraph is logged when it is added, so you can see whether it is
followed by a new entry.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] maxParagraphs = 10;
\f[B]let\f[R] count = 0;

\f[B]const\f[R] timer = setInterval(() \f[B]=>\f[R] {
  count++;
  \f[B]const\f[R] paragraph = document.createElement(\(dqp\(dq);
  paragraph.textContent = \(gaNew paragraph ${count}\(ga;
  container.appendChild(paragraph);
  log(\(gaAdded paragraph ${count}\(ga);
  \f[B]if\f[R] (count >= maxParagraphs) {
    clearInterval(timer);
  }
}, 1000);
.EE
.PP
Next we log the first time the user scrolls the scrollable
\f[CR]<div>\f[R], so you can see when reporting stops.
.IP
.EX
document.querySelector(\(dq#scroll\-box\(dq).addEventListener(
  \(dqscroll\(dq,
  () \f[B]=>\f[R] {
    log(\(dqScrolled: no further entries expected\(dq);
  },
  { once: \f[B]true\f[R] },
);
.EE
.PP
Last of all we add a click event handler to reset the example by
reloading the page.
.IP
.EX
document.querySelector(\(dq#reset\(dq).addEventListener(\(dqclick\(dq, () \f[B]=>\f[R] {
  window.location.reload();
});
.EE
.SS Result
At first, each added paragraph is followed by a new entry.
Scroll the box at any point: paragraphs are still added after that, but
no further entries are logged.
Click the \f[B]Reset\f[R] button to restart the example.
